# A Two-Note Fourier Theory of Dimension

Begin with a sparse ledger of coefficients indexed by every integer dimension. The first laboratory computes its two parity sectors and their character coordinates.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## See the grading

The spectrum distinguishes even and odd dimensions while allowing indices on both sides of zero.

{{visualization:0}}

Use the one-pass evaluation method to reproduce all four quantities.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

## Transform and reconstruct

The matrix with rows `(1,1)` and `(1,−1)` sends `(E,O)` to `(M,χ)`. Applying it twice multiplies by two.

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

Now test arbitrary coordinate pairs and discover that matching parity is the exact realizability condition.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

## Mirror symmetry

Negating a dimension never changes its parity, so the Euler signal survives reflection.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

## Products become simple

Graded products convolve parity sectors, but the total and Euler coordinates multiply independently.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

This two-channel calculation is the smallest character transform and the basic butterfly behind larger Fourier and Walsh–Hadamard methods.

## Virtual cancellation

Signed coefficients are handled by exactly the same parity transform.

{{demo:1}}

